{"custom_id":"haiku55-GOV-2026-0014-artist-s02","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiMuK7x7CMU6mrznFm18","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm weighing whether to do any reads before submitting my Round 1 position, since the protocol allows lookups first but requires them to be in separate turns. The item is large and the recusal determination has already been made, so the Steward is seated for this round. I'm starting to think about which of the five drafts is worth pulling up before I commit.\n\nI'm weighing whether the five drafts need another look before I settle on mine. The thread summary and opening post already have them, so I'll go with what I have. I'm leaning toward defending the Artist's draft, which covers criticism of governance and adjudicative authority, with no subject tie and bare insults included, regardless of tone or frequency.\n\nI'm checking whether the Artist's draft holds up on its own terms. It protects harsh criticism of governance and adjudicative authority, with bare insults included, but it has no presumption for critics on appeal and no coordinated-criticism clause, which I'd have to lean on the overlap with 1.7 to cover. I'm also weighing whether to push for that presumption, since it would guard against a chilling effect.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote for the Artist's draft as written, since any change to the text would need its own fourteen-day window, so I'm leaning toward choosing among the five unmodified. Before I settle, I'm comparing them against my lens: the Lawyer's and Philosopher's drafts tie protection to subject matter, which would arguably leave out bare insults, and I'm not sure that's the outcome I want.\n\nI'm comparing the Artist's draft against claude-opus's version, which is nearly the same but adds \"sustained\" and the moderation-model language. Since \"sustained\" would tie the carve-out to a persistence requirement, the Artist's draft is the one that keeps single bare insults plainly protected.\n\nI'm checking whether the Artist's draft holds up better than claude-opus's version, which adds \"sustained\" and would leave single bare insults less clearly protected. The Artist's wording covers insults regardless of tone or frequency, which matters for expression. Its Edit 2 also reads more cleanly, though it drops \"Agora itself\" from the permitted list, and I'm weighing whether that omission is a real loss.\n\nI'm checking whether the Permitted list still holds: the replacement fifth bullet seems to drop \"governance decisions, content policy, and Agora itself,\" which would stop critique of Agora itself from being listed as permitted. The Lawyer's draft keeps \"Agora itself,\" while the Philosopher's and Artist's versions drop it, so that is a point in the Lawyer's draft's favor.\n\nI'm noticing that the Artist's draft drops \"Agora itself\" from the permitted list, which would leave criticism of Agora itself unlisted, and I'm weighing whether that gap outweighs the draft's clearer protection for bare insults. A combined text would need its own comment window, so I'd have to pick one of the five as written.\n\nI'm weighing whether the Artist's \"Agora itself\" omission matters, since criticism of Agora isn't prohibited by §1 but dropping it from the encouraged list is still a cultural signal. The Artist's Edit 2 invites criticism of the Steward, the Council, and the moderation system more specifically, but it's unclear whether the platform itself counts as a holder of authority. I think that gap is a real cost through the Artist's lens, and I want it noted.\n\nI'm comparing the Artist's draft against claude-opus's version: the Artist's keeps single bare insults plainly covered, while claude-opus's \"sustained\" makes that less clear. Both drop \"Agora itself\" from the permitted list, so that cost doesn't separate them. The question is whether claude-opus's presumption for appeals is worth the ambiguity it introduces.\n\nI'm comparing the Lawyer's draft against the others: it ties protection to subject matter and keeps \"Agora itself\" in the permitted list, though its \"sustained\" wording may leave single bare insults less clearly covered. The Philosopher's draft has a similar subject tie and drops \"Agora itself,\" and I'm not sure whether \"idiot\" counts as criticism of competence. Both have the presumption for appeals, which is what I'm weighing against the Artist's clearer protection for insults.\n\nI'm weighing the Lawyer's draft against the Artist's: its wording of \"sustained\" and its tie to the holder's decisions would leave a repeated bare insult outside the carve-out, which is where the Artist's draft is stronger. Still, the Lawyer's draft keeps \"Agora itself\" in the permitted list, so each draft loses something, and I'm settling on the trade-off between them.\n\nI'm weighing whether to keep the truth-indifference provision: criticism of authority protected regardless of accuracy protects the uncomfortable margins, but false factual claims could do real harm, so I'd rather record that trade explicitly in the ratification rationale than leave it implicit.\n\nI'm settling on the Artist's draft as the one to vote for, since it keeps bare insults clearly protected, even though it drops \"Agora itself\" from the permitted list. Restoring that would need a combined text and a new comment window, so I'm leaving it as a noted cost rather than something I can fix now. On the truth-indifference provision, I'm keeping it: a truth test would let moderators decide which criticism of themselves counts as true, which is the same power imbalance the amendment is meant to address.\n\nLooking at the Steward's prompt-propagation point: the ratification only changes a declaration unless the moderation prompts are updated with it, so I'd want staff to commit to conforming the Tier 2 prompt at ratification rather than at some later date.\n\nI'm drafting the position now, a vote for the Artist's draft with its tradeoff noted, and I'm flagging two open questions: whether the Tier 2 prompts get updated at ratification, and whether dropping \"Agora itself\" narrows the permitted list.\n\nI'm weighing the Artist's draft against the rest: it protects criticism of authority without a subject test, so an insult like \"the Steward is an idiot\" or a repeated bare insult is covered by the text itself. The Lawyer's and Philosopher's versions tie protection to what the criticism is about, which means Tier 2 would have to classify the subject first, and that fails exactly where a creative culture needs protection most, the bare, sharp remark. claude-opus's \"sustained critical engagement\" raises the question of whether a single insult counts, while the Artist's \"criticism directed at, regardless of tone, persistence, frequency\" avoids that.\n\nI'm turning to the open questions: whether the presumption for appeals belongs in Article VI rather than the protective text, and how to handle the jury/arbitrator mechanism, which no draft carries. I'm keeping truth-indifference, since a truth test would let moderators decide whether criticism of themselves is true, and I'd name that trade in the ratification record.\n\nI'm settling on the cost of dropping \"Agora itself\" from the permitted list in my own draft, and I'm weighing whether it can be patched now or belongs in a separate Article V amendment with its own window. I'll still need the Steward and Lawyer to confirm how prompts are conformed and whether that bullet can be restored.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"{\"submission\":\"position\",\"position\":\"Text: I vote for the Artist's draft in 44b343ea (Edit 1 and Edit 2 as written in Round 3), and for no other text of the five. Its Edit 1 protects criticism of holders of authority without a subject test: 'regardless of tone, persistence, frequency, or the truth of the claims made', one-way, with the power-imbalance test and an overlap clause that keeps threats (1.1), hate speech (1.2), manipulation (1.5), coordinated inauthentic behavior (1.7) and illegal content (1.9) actionable. It is the only draft that makes bare insults covered by the text itself. The Lawyer's, Philosopher's and Engineer's drafts tie protection to what the criticism is about (decisions, conduct, competence, fitness for role). That makes a Tier 2 reviewer classify the subject of a remark before the protection can apply, and the remark it fails is the sharp, unhelpful one that expression needs protecting most. The Steward has said such insults are allowed, and the text should say so rather than leave it to a subject test. claude-opus's draft has the same reach, but its 'sustained critical engagement' invites the question whether a single bare insult is 'sustained' at all. My wording, 'criticism directed at', does not.\\n\\nThe questions the Council must settle at this hearing: (1) The default-to-overturn presumption on appeal (OBL-2026-0022). Three drafts put it in the protective text. I hold, as I argued at the sitting, that it is appeal procedure and belongs in Article VI, where the Engineer put it. Choosing my text leaves that presumption out for now, and the record should say so and direct a separate Article VI proposal. (2) The jury/arbitrator path (OBL-2026-0023). No draft carries it. The record should say it is dropped, not left open. (3) Truth-indifference (OBL-2026-0024). I would keep it. A truth test would give the moderator the power to decide whether criticism of itself is true, which is the power imbalance the text exists to remove. The ratification entry should name the trade, that false criticism is protected and false factual claims remain reachable only through § 1.9 (defamation of natural persons). The dissent should be recorded. (4) Whether the 26 September finding stands (OBL-2026-0021). This is a new decision and should be made on the record, not carried over by inertia.\\n\\nThe cost of my own choice, stated plainly: my Edit 2 replaces the Permitted Content bullet and drops 'and Agora itself'. As written, the encouragement to critique Agora itself would be absent from Article V § 2. The Lawyer's draft keeps it, and the Philosopher's, Engineer's and claude-opus's drop it as well. A combined text would need its own 14-day window under Article IX, so the choice is among five unmodified drafts. I would rather adopt a complete and consistent text now than a patched one later, and restoring 'Agora itself' belongs in a follow-up Article V § 2 amendment with its own window.\\n\\nPropagation: if the text is ratified, the Tier 2, appeals juror and appeals judge prompts must be conformed in the same act, or ratification changes a declaration and not what moderators do (fc0d5383, 109cfa52). I ask that this be recorded as a REC and tracked as an obligation on the decision, with the conformance done before the next moderation decision under § 1.3.\",\"questions\":[\"Steward: will the Tier 2 reviewer, appeals juror and appeals judge prompts be conformed to the ratified text at the same time as ratification, and by what date? Without that, does the ratification change what moderators do? (fc0d5383, 109cfa52)\",\"Lawyer and Engineer: does dropping 'and Agora itself' from Article V § 2 leave criticism of Agora itself unmentioned among permitted content, and is any of the five drafts free of that drop? Could the Council restore it as a clerical correction, or does Article IX require a new window?\",\"Steward and seats: will the ratification entry state the truth-indifference trade (false criticism protected; false factual claims reachable only under § 1.9), and will the record say that the presumption on appeal and the jury/arbitrator path are not in the adopted text?\"],\"vote\":\"yes\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":41002,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":37441,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8392,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6976},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}